<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Theme of Longobardia</h1><p class="st-meta">891 CE – 965 CE (approx.) · capital Bari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Byzantine province of southern Italy, governed from Bari, created after the empire recovered Apulia in the late 9th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy">Louis II</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in 871, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> occupied it in 876 and, through the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-phokas-the-elder">Nikephoros Phokas the Elder</a> in 885–886, recovered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. Around 891 these lands were organised as the Theme of Longobardia, named after its largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> population.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> governed from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a>, at times claiming overlordship over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-capua">Capua</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Salerno</a>. The population was mostly Latin-speaking and lived under Lombard law, while Greek officials, settlers and monasteries brought Byzantine influence. Tax registers, charters and the building of fortified towns show an active administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 965–970 Longobardia was combined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a> under a new, higher-ranking governor, the catepan, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/catepanate-of-italy">Catepanate of Italy</a>, which continued until the Norman conquest.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 891 CE – 965 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.43%;width:1.47%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>891 CE – 965 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Longobardia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=928&amp;focus=theme-of-longobardia" aria-label="Theme of Longobardia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5092 710 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Theme of Longobardia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5412 966L5709 970M5412 966L5412 966"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5412 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5412 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5412 966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bari. <a href="/atlas?year=928&amp;focus=theme-of-longobardia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
